WEBVTT

00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:01.434
AI Weekly, week 45.

00:00:01.434 --> 00:00:04.983
The weekly briefing on artificial intelligence.

00:00:04.983 --> 00:00:10.268
Created automatically with AI, not read by a human before publication.

00:00:10.268 --> 00:00:13.212
Please report errors to ai@bks-lab.com.

00:00:14.612 --> 00:00:15.728
The key points.

00:00:15.728 --> 00:00:21.534
OpenAI: The company acquires Pierre Computer and moves Codex closer to GitHub.

00:00:21.534 --> 00:00:31.581
German government: The AI task force names 18 key measures and wants 55 percent of small and medium-sized businesses to use AI by 2030.

00:00:31.581 --> 00:00:41.108
Data centers: In Dublin, around 2,000 people marched against AI data centers according to RTE and YLE, in Helsinki around 1,300.

00:00:42.508 --> 00:00:43.947
Our story of the week.

00:00:43.947 --> 00:00:47.496
OpenAI acquires Pierre Computer and builds a rival to GitHub.

00:00:47.496 --> 00:00:50.755
OpenAI has announced the acquisition of Pierre Computer.

00:00:50.755 --> 00:00:58.145
According to heise, OpenAI has already built parts of Pierre's tools into Codex, including the Git infrastructure Code Storage.

00:00:58.145 --> 00:01:06.815
With the acquisition, OpenAI may be entering another area of friction with GitHub, according to heise, and Codex is slowly approaching its functions.

00:01:06.815 --> 00:01:13.797
For context: Microsoft and OpenAI also compete in other fields, which heise describes as not unusual in the AI industry.

00:01:13.797 --> 00:01:16.125
OpenAI gave no information on the price.

00:01:16.125 --> 00:01:20.140
Pierre's open source projects remain openly accessible after closing.

00:01:20.140 --> 00:01:26.366
On the rationale, heise reports OpenAI's position as follows: the systems around Git have to keep evolving.

00:01:26.366 --> 00:01:36.723
The move hits a market that Microsoft occupies: in 2018 Microsoft bought GitHub for 7.5 billion US dollars, and Microsoft currently holds about a quarter of the shares in OpenAI.

00:01:36.723 --> 00:01:40.912
This week OpenAI also presented "Pando", a backend for Git repositories.

00:01:40.912 --> 00:01:45.626
In addition, GitHub's former CEO has launched a GitHub alternative called Entire.

00:01:45.626 --> 00:01:54.528
Critical voices: According to heise, critics say GitHub has become less reliable lately and point to an outage that disrupted Actions, Pages and billing.

00:01:54.528 --> 00:01:56.099
Heise cites them via Axios.

00:01:56.099 --> 00:01:58.427
What the acquisition costs remains open.

00:01:58.427 --> 00:02:00.114
What it means for you: Watch.

00:02:00.114 --> 00:02:09.366
Engineering leads who keep their source code on GitHub should clarify by year end which dependencies on repository, CI and coding agents they might move later.

00:02:10.766 --> 00:02:15.463
Next: German government AI task force sets targets for 2030.

00:02:15.463 --> 00:02:27.162
The German government is setting clear targets for 2030: at least 80 percent of large companies and 55 percent of small and medium-sized businesses are to use AI by then.

00:02:27.162 --> 00:02:31.154
The task force report ends with a list of 18 key measures.

00:02:31.154 --> 00:02:34.595
These are government targets, not measured values.

00:02:34.595 --> 00:02:44.368
Public administration is also meant to deliver: by 2030, 90 percent of federal agencies are to use at least one AI application for core tasks.

00:02:44.368 --> 00:02:48.979
The report warns that the window to narrow the gap is closing soon.

00:02:48.979 --> 00:02:53.452
Heise, however, considers the report to be hardly critical of AI.

00:02:53.452 --> 00:02:55.448
What it means for you: Watch.

00:02:55.448 --> 00:03:10.244
Managing directors at mid-sized companies should treat the 55 percent target as a political benchmark that depends on broad participation, and check where their own company stands today as part of their AI planning.

00:03:10.244 --> 00:03:13.902
Next: Protests against AI data centers reach Europe.

00:03:13.902 --> 00:03:21.416
In Dublin around 2,000 people marched according to the broadcaster RTE, and in Helsinki about 1,300 gathered according to YLE.

00:03:21.416 --> 00:03:27.797
On November 7, the group Stop Dirty Data Centres protested at a Microsoft data center near Køge in Denmark.

00:03:27.797 --> 00:03:31.912
No location in Germany, Austria or Switzerland appears in the report.

00:03:31.912 --> 00:03:39.962
Still, for operators and users it shows that the siting and energy demand of data centers are being negotiated in public across Europe.

00:03:39.962 --> 00:03:41.692
What it means for you: Watch.

00:03:41.692 --> 00:03:49.504
IT leads who buy data center capacity or AI services from Europe should add location and energy source to their provider selection.

00:03:50.904 --> 00:03:51.833
In brief.

00:03:51.833 --> 00:04:03.254
Briefly noted: Hacking benchmark: In the Vals test, the open model DeepSeek completes 73 percent of tasks and OpenAI's Daybreak 81 percent, measured in simulated networks.

00:04:03.254 --> 00:04:11.736
Attack costs: According to Vals, an attack with DeepSeek costs 2 to 3 US dollars in tokens, with GPT-6-Sol 17 to 46 US dollars.

00:04:11.736 --> 00:04:16.612
According to Wired, real attackers mostly use older, less capable models.

00:04:16.612 --> 00:04:28.100
NVIDIA Cosmos 3: Four new open-weight checkpoints with 16 billion parameters under the OpenMDW1.1 license, which according to NVIDIA permits commercial use under its terms.

00:04:28.100 --> 00:04:34.779
Federal agencies: By 2030, 90 percent of them are to use at least one AI application for core tasks.

00:04:36.179 --> 00:04:37.332
Tool of the week.

00:04:37.332 --> 00:04:40.601
Mistral Studio: Agents, workflows and apps on one platform.

00:04:40.601 --> 00:04:43.759
Mistral is expanding Studio with Managed Agents and Apps.

00:04:43.759 --> 00:05:01.488
According to the vendor, the agents can be deployed wherever the customer wants: "Deploy them wherever you choose: in the cloud, in your own VPC, on-premises or across a combination of environments." As references, Mistral names BNP Paribas, Accenture and Capgemini, among others, which already use Studio in production.

00:05:01.488 --> 00:05:07.638
Mistral also reports from its own teams: an internal workflow saves product operations about 35 hours per week.

00:05:07.638 --> 00:05:10.574
The reference customers named are large corporations.

00:05:10.574 --> 00:05:19.051
For European mid-sized companies that need to run agents in their own cloud or on-premises, the free choice of environment is the main point of interest.

00:05:19.051 --> 00:05:19.882
Mistral Studio.

00:05:21.282 --> 00:05:22.399
Fail of the week.

00:05:22.399 --> 00:05:25.058
Meta pauses terms change after criticism.

00:05:25.058 --> 00:05:32.452
Meta wanted to introduce new commercial terms, and after criticism the company suspended the change on October 28.

00:05:32.452 --> 00:05:42.051
According to Ars Technica, the change affects operators of business Facebook pages, who would have had to allow Meta to crawl linked external sites.

00:05:42.051 --> 00:05:49.055
The changes were originally due to apply on October 30, and Meta had already pushed the date to November 20.

00:05:49.055 --> 00:05:55.671
According to Nieman Lab, courts in Brazil and Norway issued preliminary injunctions against the terms.

00:05:55.671 --> 00:06:00.405
In addition, the UK competition authority CMA opened a formal proceeding.

00:06:00.405 --> 00:06:06.956
Cause: The terms were under pressure from courts, a regulator and the public before they took effect.

00:06:06.956 --> 00:06:12.534
Meta denies circumventing robots.txt and says its crawling practice remains unchanged.

00:06:12.534 --> 00:06:21.938
What we learn: Anyone changing terms of use for AI data should involve those affected and the regulator beforehand, or courts will stop it later.

00:06:23.338 --> 00:06:24.521
Number of the week.

00:06:24.521 --> 00:06:30.622
1.5 percent: Growth in AI token spending in August and September combined, according to Ramp data.

00:06:30.622 --> 00:06:40.146
According to Ramp data, token spending on AI grew by only 1.5 percent in August and September combined, after rising 42 percent month over month in July.

00:06:40.146 --> 00:06:44.130
In October it picked up again by 8.9 percent, according to Ramp.

00:06:44.130 --> 00:06:48.426
Ramp economist Ara Kharazian says the era of hypergrowth may be over.

00:06:48.426 --> 00:06:57.328
For context: most of the spending comes from venture-funded tech companies, so the data reflects mid-sized businesses only to a limited extent.

00:06:58.728 --> 00:07:00.717
The reading list for the weekend.

00:07:00.717 --> 00:07:10.119
For the weekend: New NVIDIA Cosmos 3 Open Models for Interactive World Simulation: Technically deep piece on open world models with license details (7 min).

00:07:10.119 --> 00:07:18.135
Free Chinese AI Models Are Getting Scary Good at Hacking: Accessible report on the Vals comparison of open and closed models (5 min).

00:07:18.135 --> 00:07:19.943
Does Meta think we can't read?

00:07:19.943 --> 00:07:26.332
Meta abandons sketchy commercial terms update: Timeline of the retreat, with courts and regulator (4 min).

00:07:27.732 --> 00:07:28.439
The outlook.

00:07:28.439 --> 00:07:34.154
Meta had most recently pushed the start of its changes to November 20 and has now suspended them.

00:07:34.154 --> 00:07:39.692
The task force targets apply until 2030, such as 55 percent AI use among mid-sized businesses.

00:07:41.092 --> 00:07:42.848
That was AI Weekly for week 45.

00:07:42.848 --> 00:07:45.058
All sources are in the written edition.

00:07:45.058 --> 00:07:45.964
Until next week.

